Q: How to automatically use &quot;product title&quot; as focus keyword in rank math? A: <p>How to automatically update the focus keyword with the post title and page title, if no focus keyword is set. Please help.</p>
<p>When I add the product code above, it works great, but when I add code for posts and pages, I get errors.</p>
<p>How can I have this code automate products, post and pages to work all together?</p>
<p>When posting a post and creating pages, I would like the auto focus keyword to automatically set the title for the focus keyword.</p>
<p>Thanks</p>
<p>When trying to add the code below for the post, page, and products, I get errors.</p>
<p>Please help.</p>
<pre><code>function update_focus_keywords() {
    $posts = get_posts(array(
    'posts_per_page'    =&gt; -1,
    'post_type'     =&gt; 'product','post', 'pages'  // Replace post with the name of your post type
    ));
    foreach($posts as $p){
        // Checks if Rank Math keyword already exists and only updates if it doesn't have it
        $rank_math_keyword = get_post_meta( $p-&gt;ID, 'rank_math_focus_keyword', true );
    if ( ! $rank_math_keyword ){ 
            update_post_meta($p-&gt;ID,'rank_math_focus_keyword',strtolower(get_the_title($p-&gt;ID)));
        }
    }
}

add_filter( 'rank_math/frontend/description', function( $description ) {
    if ( is_product() ) {
        global $post;
        $short_desc = get_post_meta( $post-&gt;ID, '_short_description', true );
        return $short_desc ? $short_desc . ' | Order today!' : get_the_title( $post-&gt;ID ) . ' – Shop now!';
    }
    return $description;
});
</code></pre>

Q: Creating Scheduled Tasks A: <p>If you do not want to use any third party libraries, you can add &quot;TaskScheduler 1.1&quot; as a COM reference.</p>
<h3>Adding the reference:</h3>
<p>Solution Explorer -&gt; Pick the project -&gt; Right Click on References -&gt; Add Reference</p>
<p>Select &quot;COM&quot;, then select the &quot;TaskScheduler 1.1&quot; type library.</p>
<h3>Interfaces used</h3>
<p><code>ITaskService</code> - Object created first, used for creating blank tasks, or getting a task folder<br />
<code>ITaskFolder</code> - Used for registering a new task or getting an existing task<br />
<code>ITaskDefinition</code> - The object for the task itself.  Fill in the fields when creating, or read them from an existing task.<br />
<code>IExecAction</code> - When getting or setting an EXE for the task, you need to cast an action to this type.</p>
<h3>Creating the task service</h3>
<p>To create an instance of the task service:</p>
<pre><code>var typeOfTaskService = Type.GetTypeFromCLSID(new Guid(&quot;0F87369F-A4E5-4CFC-BD3E-73E6154572DD&quot;));
var taskService = (ITaskService)Activator.CreateInstance(typeOfTaskService);
</code></pre>
<h3>Getting the root directory</h3>
<p>Use <code>taskService.GetFolder(&quot;\\&quot;)</code> to get the task scheduler's root directory.  If you don't want to use the root directory, you can use something else.</p>
<h3>Creating the task and giving it an action:</h3>
<p>You use the <code>ITaskService</code> class to create new empty tasks, and the <code>ITaskFolder</code> class to register the task in that particular folder.</p>
<p>When you create an action for the class, it goes into an Actions collection, and after creating the action, you need to cast it to another type, as shown here:</p>
<pre><code>var taskDefinition = taskService.NewTask(0);
var actionColllection = taskDefinition.Actions;
var action = actionColllection.Create(_TASK_ACTION_TYPE.TASK_ACTION_EXEC);
var execAction = (IExecAction)action;
execAction.Path = exePath;
</code></pre>
<p>Setting <code>taskDefinition.Principal</code> to determine what user and permissions it runs at:</p>
<pre><code>var principal = taskDefinition.Principal;

//running as Admin for all users (for a auto-starting task)
principal.RunLevel = _TASK_RUNLEVEL.TASK_RUNLEVEL_HIGHEST;
principal.GroupId = &quot;Users&quot;;
principal.UserId = null;

//running as Admin for current logged-in user (for an auto-starting or on-demand task)
principal.RunLevel = _TASK_RUNLEVEL.TASK_RUNLEVEL_HIGHEST;
principal.GroupId = null;
principal.UserId = Environment.UserDomainName + &quot;\\&quot; + Environment.UserName;
</code></pre>
<p>Setting the trigger for the task:</p>
<pre><code>var triggers = taskDefinition.Triggers;
var trigger = triggers.Create(_TASK_TRIGGER_TYPE2.TASK_TRIGGER_LOGON);
</code></pre>
<p>Setting <code>taskDefinition.Settings</code> to pick settings for the task (you do not want the defaults)</p>
<pre><code>var taskSettings = taskDefinition.Settings;
taskSettings.AllowDemandStart = true;
taskSettings.AllowHardTerminate = true;
taskSettings.Compatibility = _TASK_COMPATIBILITY.TASK_COMPATIBILITY_V2_1;
taskSettings.DisallowStartIfOnBatteries = false;
taskSettings.Enabled = true;
taskSettings.RunOnlyIfIdle = false;
taskSettings.RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable = false;
taskSettings.StopIfGoingOnBatteries = false;
taskSettings.ExecutionTimeLimit = &quot;PT0S&quot;;
taskSettings.MultipleInstances = _TASK_INSTANCES_POLICY.TASK_INSTANCES_STOP_EXISTING;
taskDefinition.Settings = taskSettings;
</code></pre>
<p>Finally register the task (must be Admin to do this):</p>
<pre><code>var rootFolder = taskService.GetFolder(&quot;\\&quot;);
const int TASK_CREATE_OR_UPDATE = 6;
var registeredTask = rootFolder.RegisterTaskDefinition(taskName, taskDefinition, TASK_CREATE_OR_UPDATE, null, null, _TASK_LOGON_TYPE.TASK_LOGON_INTERACTIVE_TOKEN, null);
</code></pre>

Q: PHP sentence case function A: <p>Here is a simple function which examines the sentence endigs in any string (that is full stops, exclamation and question marks, as well as possible zero-, single- or double-space sentence starts, and then capitalizes the first letter following such endings. it also takes account of line breaks.<br />
If an unusual sentence ending crops up then simply add it to the ARRAY $endings.</p>
<pre><code>function sentenceCase($string){
    // To prevent a &quot;Canno redeclare&quot; error
    if (!function_exists(&quot;capitalizeAfter&quot;))
    {
        function capitalizeAfter($str, $find){
            // Initialise a counter
            $lastPos = 0;
            $offset = strlen($find);
            // While a |find| string is found...
            while (($lastPos = strpos($str, $find, $lastPos))!== false) 
            {
                // Extract the first letter after the |find| string
                $l =  substr($str, $lastPos + $offset, 1);
                // If NOT upper case then make it upper case and replace
                if($l != strtoupper($l))
                {
                    $l = strtoupper($l);
                    $str = substr_replace($str, $l, $lastPos + $offset, 1);
                }
                // Increment the counter
                $lastPos = $lastPos + strlen($find) ;
            }
            // RETURN the adjusted string to the caller
            return $str;
        };

    }

    // Possible sentence endings
    $endings = [
        &quot;&lt;br&gt;&quot;, &quot;&lt;br&gt; &quot;, &quot;&lt;br&gt;  &quot;, &quot;. &quot;, &quot;.  &quot;, &quot;?&quot;, &quot;? &quot;, &quot;!&quot;, &quot;! &quot;
    ];

    // Change \n to &lt;br&gt;
    $string = nl2br($string);

    // Capitalize after every possible sentence ending
    foreach($endings as $e)
    {
        $string = capitalizeAfter($string, $e);
    }

    // Captialise first letter
    $string = ucfirst($string);

    // RETURN adjusted string to caller
    return $string;
};
</code></pre>
